<!-- ==================== ABSTRACT ==================== -->
<h2 class="text-center" style="margin-bottom: 15pt; text-decoration: underline;">ABSTRACT</h2>

<p style="line-height: 1.7; text-indent: 30pt;">
The <strong>Clinic Appointment Scheduling System</strong> is a full-stack, enterprise-grade medical consultation booking and clinical queue management platform engineered to resolve critical operational bottlenecks encountered in municipal healthcare centers, private outpatient departments (OPDs), and multi-speciality polyclinics. Traditional physical healthcare centers rely on manual paper registers, informal telephone bookings, and unstructured walk-in queues. These manual mechanisms suffer from catastrophic scheduling conflicts (such as double-booking doctors for the same consultation hour), inaccurate record-keeping, extensive patient waiting times, lack of digital auditability, and unauthorized access to medical records.
</p>

<p style="line-height: 1.7; text-indent: 30pt; margin-top: 10pt;">
To eliminate these inefficiencies, this project introduces a secure, high-concurrency, database-driven web platform constructed strictly using <strong>Java 21</strong>, <strong>Jakarta Servlets 6.0</strong>, <strong>Jakarta Server Pages (JSP 3.1)</strong>, <strong>JDBC</strong>, and <strong>MySQL 8.0</strong>, deployed on <strong>Apache Tomcat 10.1</strong>. 
</p>

<p style="line-height: 1.7; text-indent: 30pt; margin-top: 10pt;">
The system implements a distinct, dual-role architecture:
</p>

<p style="line-height: 1.7; margin-left: 20pt;">
1. <strong>Patient Subsystem:</strong> Allows citizens and patients to register their demographic and medical profiles (<code>patient_id</code>, <code>patient_name</code>, <code>dob</code>, <code>gender</code>, <code>phone</code>, <code>email</code>, <code>password</code>, <code>blood_group</code>, <code>address</code>). Patients authenticate using either their unique Patient ID or registered Email ID. The patient portal provides real-time doctor directory discovery across multiple clinical specializations (Cardiology, Pediatrics, General Medicine, Dermatology, Orthopedics, Gynecology). An intelligent appointment booking engine enables patients to select consultation dates and 30-minute time slots while describing symptoms. Crucially, the booking engine enforces <strong>atomic concurrency protection</strong> via database row-level locking (<code>SELECT ... FOR UPDATE</code>) and composite unique constraints (<code>UNIQUE KEY (doctor_id, appointment_date, time_slot)</code>), mathematically preventing two patients from booking the same doctor and slot simultaneously. Patients can track consultation statuses (<code>Pending</code> &rarr; <code>Confirmed</code> &rarr; <code>Completed</code> / <code>Cancelled</code>), cancel upcoming visits, and generate/print official <strong>Digital Medical Appointment Slips</strong> with unique alphanumeric tracking tokens (<code>APT-YYYY-XXXX</code>).
</p>

<p style="line-height: 1.7; margin-left: 20pt; margin-top: 8pt;">
2. <strong>Receptionist Subsystem:</strong> Serves front-desk healthcare administrators with a high-visibility command center. The dashboard presents real-time key performance indicators (Total Bookings, Today's Consultations, Pending Verifications, Confirmed Schedules, Cancelled Appointments). Receptionists can filter and sort patient queues dynamically by consultation date, assigned doctor, and current status. With a single click, receptionists can verify and confirm pending schedules, cancel slots (releasing them back into the availability pool), or check in patients upon consultation completion, while maintaining a strict audit trail in <code>appointment_audit_log</code>.
</p>

<p style="line-height: 1.7; text-indent: 30pt; margin-top: 10pt;">
Security and access control are guaranteed by two dedicated Jakarta servlet filters (<code>PatientAuthFilter</code> and <code>ReceptionistAuthFilter</code>), preventing unauthenticated URL snooping and enforcing complete role separation. The application strictly adheres to the <strong>Model-View-Controller (MVC)</strong> architectural pattern and <strong>Data Access Object (DAO)</strong> pattern, delivering a secure, responsive, and robust healthcare platform.
</p>

