<!-- ==================== CHAPTER 1 ==================== -->
<h2>1. INTRODUCTION</h2>

<h3>1.1 Background of the Problem</h3>
<p>
Healthcare delivery in developing urban centers and suburban towns (such as Mylavaram and Vijayawada) has expanded exponentially over the last decade. Polyclinics and multi-speciality outpatient health facilities handle hundreds of patients daily. However, a significant majority of clinics continue to manage appointments through physical register books, telephone calls, or disorderly walk-in lines.
</p>
<p>
This traditional approach introduces severe operational bottlenecks:
</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Double-Booking Conflicts:</strong> Manual entry frequently results in two or more patients being assigned the same doctor during the exact same time slot, leading to physician exhaustion, delayed consultations, and severe patient frustration.</li>
    <li><strong>Prolonged Waiting Times:</strong> Unscheduled walk-in crowds cause unpredictable congestion in waiting rooms, exposing vulnerable patients (especially children, pregnant women, and elderly citizens) to nosocomial infections.</li>
    <li><strong>Absence of Real-Time Visibility:</strong> Patients have no means to know whether a doctor is available, on leave, or delayed before physically arriving at the hospital.</li>
    <li><strong>Data Errors and Missing Records:</strong> Hand-written records lead to misplaced demographic details, phone numbers, and previous consultation notes.</li>
    <li><strong>Lack of Auditability:</strong> Traditional desks have no automated logging mechanism to track which staff member confirmed, rescheduled, or cancelled a patient's appointment.</li>
</ul>

<h3>1.2 Objectives of the System</h3>
<p>
The primary objective of the <strong>Clinic Appointment Scheduling System</strong> is to design, develop, and deploy an automated, secure, and intuitive web application that modernizes the outpatient consultation lifecycle:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>Provide patients with a seamless, 24/7 self-service portal to browse specialists, check consultation cabin details and fees, and reserve consultation slots.</li>
    <li>Implement deterministic concurrency control at both the application and database tiers to eliminate double-booking for any doctor and time slot.</li>
    <li>Enable receptionists and front-desk personnel to monitor the master clinic schedule, filter appointments by date and medical specialist, and update appointment statuses (<code>Pending</code> &rarr; <code>Confirmed</code> &rarr; <code>Completed</code> / <code>Cancelled</code>) with automated audit tracking.</li>
    <li>Automatically generate an official, printable <strong>Digital Medical Appointment Slip / Token</strong> featuring doctor cabin numbers, patient demographics, and instructions to expedite OPD check-in.</li>
    <li>Enforce robust security, role-based access control, parameterized database access, and session integrity.</li>
</ul>

<h3>1.3 Scope of the Application</h3>
<p>
The scope encompasses all primary outpatient scheduling activities across multi-speciality departments (Cardiology, Pediatrics, General Medicine, Dermatology, Orthopedics, and Gynecology):
</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>In Scope:</strong> Patient registration and profile management, dual-credential authentication (Patient ID / Email), doctor directory discovery, real-time slot conflict detection, front-desk dashboard with date/doctor/status filters, status transition persistence in MySQL, audit logging, printable appointment tokens, and session-based access control filters.</li>
    <li><strong>Out of Scope (Future Phases):</strong> Inpatient (IPD) bed allocation, integration with commercial SMS gateways, electronic health record (EHR) diagnostic imaging storage, and third-party commercial payment gateways (the system is optimized for direct on-desk clinic fee payment).</li>
</ul>

<h3>1.4 Feasibility Study</h3>
<p>
Before initiating system development, an in-depth feasibility assessment was conducted across three essential axes:
</p>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Technical Feasibility:</strong> The system is architected using enterprise-grade, mature, and open-source Java technologies: Java 21 LTS, Jakarta Servlets 6.0, JSP 3.1, and MySQL 8.0, running on Apache Tomcat 10.1. These platforms are renowned for rock-solid stability, cross-platform portability, high concurrent throughput, and robust multi-threading capabilities. Concurrency protection is achieved using native MySQL InnoDB row-level locking (<code>SELECT ... FOR UPDATE</code>) and composite unique constraints, requiring no external complex messaging middleware.</li>
    <li><strong>Operational Feasibility:</strong> The user interface is built using modern Bootstrap 5.3 and custom CSS adhering to a clinical healthcare palette (Trust Blue <code>#0284c7</code>, Teal <code>#0d9488</code>, Clean White <code>#ffffff</code>, and Slate <code>#0f172a</code>). Patients of varying digital literacy can easily select a doctor, choose a date and time slot with intuitive pill buttons, and receive an instant token within four clicks. Receptionists are provided with a dedicated command console with real-time KPI metric cards and one-click confirm/cancel action triggers.</li>
    <li><strong>Economic Feasibility:</strong> The entire technological stack utilized—including Java OpenJDK, Apache Tomcat, MySQL Community Edition, Apache Maven, and Bootstrap—is free and open-source, eliminating costly commercial software licensing. The application runs smoothly on standard low-cost commodity hardware or local on-premises clinic servers, making it economically accessible to rural and semi-urban medical centers.</li>
</ol>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ==================== CHAPTER 2 ==================== -->
<h2>2. TECHNOLOGIES USED</h2>

<h3>2.1 Frontend: HTML5, CSS3, JavaScript &amp; Bootstrap 5.3</h3>
<p>
The presentation layer delivers a fast, responsive, and aesthetically pleasing interface across mobile devices, tablets, and desktop workstations:
</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>HTML5:</strong> Semantic document structuring (<code>&lt;header&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;section&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;footer&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;form&gt;</code>) ensures cross-browser accessibility and structured data presentation.</li>
    <li><strong>CSS3 (<code>clinic-style.css</code>):</strong> Custom healthcare stylesheet establishing visual hierarchy. Features CSS variables for the color palette, card hover elevation, pill status badges, and print-specific stylesheets (<code>@media print</code>) that format medical appointment slips cleanly on standard A4 paper while suppressing navigation bars and buttons.</li>
    <li><strong>JavaScript (ES6+):</strong> Provides client-side dynamic interactivity: dynamic calendar restrictions (enforcing <code>min = today</code> to disallow historical dates), interactive time slot button toggles, asynchronous parameter passing, and confirmation modal dialogs for appointment cancellations.</li>
    <li><strong>Bootstrap 5.3.3 &amp; Bootstrap Icons 1.11.3:</strong> Delivers responsive grid layouts, fluid tables, metric cards, navigation bars, and medical iconography without dependency on bulky third-party libraries.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2.2 Backend: Java 21, Jakarta Servlets 6.0 &amp; Apache Tomcat 10.1</h3>
<p>
The backend controller and business logic rely on the latest enterprise Java standards:
</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Java 21 LTS:</strong> Serves as the core programming platform, offering enhanced language capabilities, strong type safety, superior memory management, and modern garbage collection performance.</li>
    <li><strong>Jakarta Servlets 6.0:</strong> Implements the Controller tier under the MVC paradigm. Servlets extend <code>HttpServlet</code>, intercepting incoming HTTP <code>GET</code> and <code>POST</code> requests, parsing request parameters, coordinating with the Data Access Object (DAO) layer, binding session attributes, and redirecting or dispatching to JSP views.</li>
    <li><strong>Apache Tomcat 10.1:</strong> An enterprise-ready, open-source HTTP servlet container compliant with the Jakarta EE 10 specification. It efficiently manages worker thread pools, handles concurrent client connections, and maintains <code>HttpSession</code> lifecycles.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2.3 Persistence: MySQL 8.0, JDBC &amp; ACID Transactions</h3>
<p>
Data storage is governed strictly by the relational model:
</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>MySQL 8.0 Database Engine:</strong> Relational storage powered by the InnoDB engine, providing full ACID (Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability) guarantees, relational foreign keys with cascading options, and specialized unique index structures.</li>
    <li><strong>Java Database Connectivity (JDBC):</strong> Provides low-overhead direct database communication via <code>com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Parameterized <code>PreparedStatement</code>:</strong> All SQL operations strictly use parameterized queries where user inputs are bound via typed setter methods (<code>setString()</code>, <code>setInt()</code>, <code>setDate()</code>), eliminating all vectors of SQL Injection attacks.</li>
    <li><strong>Pessimistic Concurrency &amp; Transactions:</strong> The critical booking transaction turns off autocommit (<code>conn.setAutoCommit(false)</code>), executes a locked verification query (<code>SELECT ... FOR UPDATE</code>), performs the insertion, records the audit trail, and commits the transaction atomically.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2.4 Presentation &amp; Security: JSP 3.1, JSTL 3.0 &amp; BCrypt</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Jakarta Server Pages (JSP 3.1) &amp; JSTL 3.0:</strong> Delivers dynamic server-side rendering while preserving strict separation of concerns. JSTL core tags (<code>&lt;c:forEach&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;c:if&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;c:choose&gt;</code>) iterate over domain model lists and render conditional status indicators without embedding raw Java scriptlets.</li>
    <li><strong>BCrypt Password Security (<code>jBCrypt 0.4</code>):</strong> Implements adaptive one-way cryptographic hashing for passwords, combining random salt generation with key-stretching work factors.</li>
    <li><strong>Role-Based Authentication Filters:</strong> <code>PatientAuthFilter</code> and <code>ReceptionistAuthFilter</code> guard protected application paths, verifying session attributes and invalidating cookies on logout (<code>session.invalidate()</code>).</li>
</ul>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ==================== CHAPTER 3 ==================== -->
<h2>3. WEB APPLICATION ARCHITECTURE</h2>

<h3>3.1 Three-Tier MVC Architecture Model</h3>
<p>
The application architecture is cleanly decoupled into three foundational tiers:
</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Presentation Tier (View):</strong> Developed using JSP 3.1, JSTL, Bootstrap 5.3, and custom CSS. Provides views for Patient Login, Registration, Doctor Catalog, Slot Booking Form, Appointments Table, Digital Slip, and Receptionist Command Console.</li>
    <li><strong>Application Tier (Controller &amp; Logic):</strong> Powered by Java 21 and Jakarta Servlets 6.0 running on Apache Tomcat 10.1. Handles authentication, routing, validation, token generation, and coordination between the frontend and database.</li>
    <li><strong>Data Persistence Tier (DAO &amp; Database):</strong> Built on MySQL 8.0 with InnoDB engine. Data Access Objects (<code>PatientDAO</code>, <code>DoctorDAO</code>, <code>AppointmentDAO</code>, <code>ReceptionistDAO</code>) encapsulate all SQL execution and transaction boundaries.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3.2 Database Relational Schema Design</h3>
<p>The relational database <code>clinic_appointment_db</code> comprises 5 structured tables:</p>
<ol>
    <li><strong>patients:</strong> Stores patient profiles with primary key <code>patient_id</code>, full name, date of birth, gender, contact phone, unique email address, password hash, blood group, and residential address.</li>
    <li><strong>doctors:</strong> Stores medical specialist details with primary key <code>doctor_id</code>, name, clinical specialization, qualifications, experience years, allocated OPD room number, consultation fee, available working days, and consultation hours.</li>
    <li><strong>receptionists:</strong> Stores authorized hospital staff credentials with primary key <code>username</code>, password hash, staff full name, official email, and phone.</li>
    <li><strong>appointments:</strong> The central transactional entity linking patients and doctors. Contains primary key <code>appointment_id</code>, unique token <code>appointment_token</code>, foreign key <code>patient_id</code>, foreign key <code>doctor_id</code>, scheduled consultation date, time slot, patient chief complaint, status (<code>Pending</code>, <code>Confirmed</code>, <code>Cancelled</code>, <code>Completed</code>), and timestamps. Contains composite unique key:
    <div class="code-block">CONSTRAINT uq_doctor_slot UNIQUE (doctor_id, appointment_date, time_slot)</div>
    </li>
    <li><strong>appointment_audit_log:</strong> Comprehensive audit trail tracking status alterations, actions, performing user IDs, timestamps, and staff remarks.</li>
</ol>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ==================== CHAPTER 4 ==================== -->
<h2>4. CODING &amp; IMPLEMENTATION</h2>

<h3>4.1 Concurrency &amp; Double-Booking Protection</h3>
<p>
Double-booking is mathematically prevented using a two-stage defense mechanism:
</p>
<p>
<strong>Stage 1 (Pessimistic Application-Level Locking):</strong> In <code>AppointmentDAO.bookAppointment()</code>, before performing any insertion, the connection autocommit is disabled. A locked query is executed against the database:
</p>
<div class="code-block">
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM appointments <br>
WHERE doctor_id = ? AND appointment_date = ? AND time_slot = ? <br>
AND status != 'Cancelled' FOR UPDATE;
</div>
<p>
If the count is greater than zero, the transaction is immediately rolled back and the method returns <code>false</code>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>Stage 2 (Database-Level Composite Unique Constraint):</strong> Even under extreme thread race conditions, the MySQL engine guarantees integrity via the table-level constraint:
</p>
<div class="code-block">
CONSTRAINT uq_doctor_slot UNIQUE (doctor_id, appointment_date, time_slot);
</div>
<p>
Any concurrent duplicate thread attempting an insert will trigger a MySQL <code>SQLException (Error 1062 - Duplicate entry)</code>, causing an immediate transaction rollback without corrupting schedule integrity.
</p>

<h3>4.2 Key Source Code Implementation</h3>

<h4>4.2.1 Database Connection Utility (`DBConnectionUtil.java`)</h4>
<div class="code-block">
public class DBConnectionUtil {<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;private static String url = "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/clinic_appointment_db?useSSL=false&allowPublicKeyRetrieval=true&serverTimezone=UTC";<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;private static String username = "root";<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;private static String password = "Hema@123";<br>
<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;static {<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;try {<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Class.forName("com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver");<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;} catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;e.printStackTrace();<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;}<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;}<br>
<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;public static Connection getConnection() throws SQLException {<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;return DriverManager.getConnection(url, username, password);<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;}<br>
}
</div>

<h4>4.2.2 Patient Authentication Servlet (`PatientLoginServlet.java`)</h4>
<div class="code-block">
@WebServlet("/patientLogin")<br>
public class PatientLoginServlet extends HttpServlet {<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;private PatientDAO patientDAO = new PatientDAO();<br>
<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;protected void doPost(HttpServletRequest req, HttpServletResponse resp) throws ServletException, IOException {<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;String idOrEmail = req.getParameter("idOrEmail");<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;String password = req.getParameter("password");<br>
<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Patient patient = patientDAO.authenticate(idOrEmail, password);<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;if (patient != null) {<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;HttpSession session = req.getSession(true);<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;session.setAttribute("patient", patient);<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;resp.sendRedirect(req.getContextPath() + "/patientHome");<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;} else {<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;req.setAttribute("errorMessage", "Invalid Patient ID / Email or Password.");<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;req.getRequestDispatcher("/patient_login.jsp").forward(req, resp);<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;}<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;}<br>
}
</div>

