<!-- ==================== CHAPTER 6 ==================== -->
<h2>6. CONCLUSION &amp; FUTURE SCOPE</h2>

<h3>6.1 Conclusion</h3>
<p>
The <strong>Clinic Appointment Scheduling System</strong> successfully bridges the gap between traditional manual clinical record-keeping and contemporary digital healthcare management. Built from scratch adhering to the mandatory college technology stack (<strong>Java 21</strong>, <strong>Jakarta Servlets 6.0</strong>, <strong>JSP 3.1</strong>, <strong>JDBC</strong>, and <strong>MySQL 8.0</strong> on <strong>Apache Tomcat 10.1</strong>), the system satisfies all operational criteria specified in Case Study 2.
</p>
<p>
The implementation of two-tier concurrency control ensures zero double-booking occurrences for any doctor, protecting clinic staff from scheduling chaos. The clear separation of concerns across the MVC Architecture and DAO Pattern guarantees high maintainability, code readability, and security. Front-desk personnel benefit from real-time queue visibility, date/doctor filtering, and instant status updates, while patients enjoy an intuitive self-service portal with digital appointment tokens.
</p>

<h3>6.2 Future Scope</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Automated SMS &amp; WhatsApp Alerts:</strong> Integration with clinical SMS gateways to send automated appointment confirmation tokens and reminder alerts 2 hours prior to consultation.</li>
    <li><strong>Telemedicine &amp; Video Consultation:</strong> WebRTC-based video conferencing allowing patients to consult specialists remotely.</li>
    <li><strong>Electronic Health Records (EHR):</strong> Digital prescription module enabling doctors to record diagnostic findings, lab test prescriptions, and medicine schedules directly onto the patient's profile.</li>
    <li><strong>Emergency Triage Queue:</strong> Priority scoring for acute emergency visits to dynamically adjust routine appointment intervals.</li>
</ul>

